    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nFor a polytropic process $P V^n = \\text{const}$, $C = C_v + \\frac{R}{1 - n}$ and $T V^{n - 1} = \\text{const}$.\n\n2. GIVEN:\nMonoatomic gas ($C_v = \\frac{3}{2} R$), $n = 2$, initial state $(T_0, V_0)$, final volume $2V_0$.\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\nQ = n C \\Delta T\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. Molar heat capacity: $C = \\frac{3}{2} R + \\frac{R}{1 - 2} = \\frac{3}{2} R - R = \\frac{1}{2} R$.\n2. Temperature variation: $T V^{2 - 1} = \\text{constant} \\implies T V = \\text{constant}$.\n3. Final temperature: $T_f (2V_0) = T_0 V_0 \\implies T_f = \\frac{T_0}{2}$.\n4. Temperature change: $\\Delta T = T_f - T_0 = -\\frac{T_0}{2}$.\n5. Heat absorbed: $Q = 1 \\times C \\times \\Delta T = \\left(\\frac{1}{2} R\\right) \\left(-\\frac{T_0}{2}\\right) = -\\frac{1}{4} R T_0$.\n6. If magnitude of heat released is asked or depending on sign: $|Q| = \\frac{1}{4} R T_0$.\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: $\\frac{1}{4} R T_0$\n\n6. WHY OTHER OPTIONS ARE INCORRECT:\nOption D assumes adiabatic conditions ($n = \\gamma$); here $n = 2 \\neq 5/3$.",
